{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And every firstborn shall die.</b> And He did not say ״and I will kill every״ etc., for HaShem blessed be He performs the good with His own hand, but the work of evil He commands to His angels, the doers of His word, and they perform it. Therefore He did not say ״and I will kill״ but rather ״and shall die״ — meaning, by means of an agent. And the act of HaShem — this is the arousing of the consciousness of the firstborns, and this causes the destroyer to destroy him and he dies. And from this the meaning of His saying ״and I will smite״ will be revealed, which is likewise by means of an agent." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 34a): יָהַב בֵּיהּ עֵינֵיהּ וְקַטְלֵיהּ — 'he set his eye upon him and killed him'; and they also said: וְנַעֲשָׂה גַּל שֶׁל עֲצָמוֹת — 'and he was made into a heap of bones.' Now these statements, at first glance, the intellect would distance, for how could the gaze of a righteous man be for evil? And is it not written (Proverbs 22:9): ״טוֹב עַיִן הוּא יְבֹרָךְ״ — 'a good eye, he shall be blessed'; and Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai, of blessed memory, also noted (Zohar III 211a) concerning the evil eye of the injurer with his eye, that it is the evil portion. However, after closer examination of what they said — יָהִיב עֵינֵיהּ — it should have said רָאָה בּוֹ בְּעֵינָיו ('he saw him with his eyes'); but the reason is that every evil portion in the world of necessity must have something that gives it whatever minimal vitality it has, which is the aspect of the good, for the evil portion is called death — so how can it live and exist, let alone move and behave like living beings? Therefore it necessarily contains some portion, however small, from the dimension called life, and this portion is good. And with this you will understand their saying, of blessed memory (Sukkah 52a), that in the future the Holy One blessed be He will bring the Angel of Death (Samael) and slaughter him in the presence of the righteous, etc., end quote. And these words have no [literal] meaning, for slaughter cannot be applied to an angel; and according to what we have prefaced, the intention will be that He removes from him the portion that gives him life, and with the removal of the good portion from him — this is his slaughter." "<b>Further,</b> you should know that every source yearns for its own kind and draws it in. And this is the sod of the dimension of the refinement of the holy sparks through the agency of the souls of Israel and their engagement in Torah. And the mighty righteous ones, our predecessors, would perceive, when they looked upon a wicked person, [how] to refine from him the vital force, which is the aspect of the good, through the subtle act of gazing which they performed with the eye of wisdom, to extract that good portion. For when they intend toward the branch of holiness, the soul of the righteous acts upon him like the action of the lodestone drawing iron — called calaimite in the foreign tongue (la'az) — which draws it out from the place where it was fixed by [the act of] gazing. And HaShem created likenesses in the world to establish a person's faith in the Torah of the wise. From this we find the divine wisdom in His saying ״and every firstborn shall die״ — meaning that through my passing through the midst of Egypt, every firstborn will die of itself, analogous to the gaze of the sages upon the wicked, who turn them into heaps of bones, for through their gazing the vitality departs from them. So too this matter, which separates [the good] from them through the agency of His passing there — the passing of all the souls of the firstborns. And understand these matters." "And His saying <b>every firstborn</b> — meaning, not that the soul will depart from the body alone, but rather that even the dimension of soul shall die: the body and soul of the kelipah of the Egyptian firstborn. And perhaps it is for this reason that no exile was ever as intense as the dimension of the exile and servitude of Egypt, for the mighty force of the kelipah, which is the dimension of the firstborn, was destroyed." "<b>It appeared proper to me</b> to give a reason why Israel did not depart except through the agency of the plague of the firstborns. And further, why did HaShem strike even a firstborn who was not Egyptian, as it is written (Exodus 12:29): ״בְּכוֹר הַשְּׁבִי״ — 'the firstborn of the captive.' And perhaps the reason is because we find that HaShem called Israel (Exodus 4:22): ״בְּנִי בְכוֹרִי״ — 'My son, My firstborn'; and our Sages, of blessed memory, have already informed us (Zohar II 63a) that everything the Holy One blessed be He created with the dimension of good, the Elohim likewise made corresponding to it in the dimension of evil, and every dimension that exists in holiness has its counterpart in the kelipah, and the kelipah exerts effort and strengthens to hold fast to it. Therefore, corresponding to the dimension of firstbornhood that is in holiness, there was a dimension of firstbornhood in the kelipah gripping and asserting force over it so as not to let go, until HaShem killed every name of firstbornhood in the kelipah of Egypt — whether of the Egyptians or of the other nations who were there, as it is written: ״עַד בְּכוֹר הַשְּׁבִי״ and ״בְּכוֹר הַשִּׁפְחָה״ and ״וְכׁל בְּכוֹר בְּהֵמָה״. And also the firstborn of Israel HaShem sanctified, as He said (Numbers 8:17): ״כָּל בְּכוֹר״ etc. ״הִקְדַּשְׁתִּי אוֹתָם לִי״ — 'every firstborn... I have sanctified them to Myself,' so that no firstbornhood would remain except that of holiness which HaShem had sanctified. And therefore even the firstborn of animals died; and since this name was uprooted, the evil branch that had been holding fast to the firstborn of holiness fell, and therefore the children of Israel departed immediately. And although we find that they went out and pursued them, they thought that they still had power to hold them; but go out and learn what came of their effort — not one remained."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "In this comment, the phrase הזכרת הודעת הבכורות (literally 'the mentioning/awakening of the informing of the firstborns') is rendered as 'the arousing of the consciousness of the firstborns.' The idiom is unusual and rendered according to context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:00.153222139Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}